  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "Say 'don't' — and give orders politely",
    "intro": "Last unit you learned <b>“Habla!”</b> for a friend. But to say <b>“Don't speak!”</b> — or to give an order to someone you address as <b>usted</b> — Spanish switches to a special form. Both use the <b>subjunctive</b> command endings: flip the vowel of the verb.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "negativas",
        "heading": "Negative tú: flip the vowel",
        "body": "For negative <b>tú</b> commands, take the yo form, drop the <b>-o</b>, and add the <b>opposite</b> vowel: <b>-ar</b> verbs take <b>-es</b>, and <b>-er/-ir</b> verbs take <b>-as</b>:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "hablar",
            "no hables"
          ],
          [
            "comer",
            "no comas"
          ],
          [
            "escribir",
            "no escribas"
          ],
          [
            "ir",
            "no vayas"
          ],
          [
            "hacer",
            "no hagas"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>No hables</b> tan rápido.",
            "en": "Don't speak so fast.",
            "note": "hablar → no hables"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>No vayas</b> sin mí.",
            "en": "Don't go without me.",
            "note": "ir → no vayas"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "formal",
        "heading": "Formal usted commands",
        "body": "For someone you address as <b>usted</b>, use the same subjunctive form for both <b>affirmative</b> and <b>negative</b>. The <b>-ar</b> verbs end in <b>-e</b>, the <b>-er/-ir</b> verbs in <b>-a</b>:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "hablar",
            "hable"
          ],
          [
            "comer",
            "coma"
          ],
          [
            "venir",
            "venga"
          ],
          [
            "hacer",
            "haga"
          ],
          [
            "hablar (neg.)",
            "no hable"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Hable</b> más despacio, por favor.",
            "en": "Speak more slowly, please.",
            "note": "polite: usted"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>Venga</b> por aquí, señor.",
            "en": "Come this way, sir.",
            "note": "venir → venga"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "contraste",
        "heading": "Friend vs. formal, side by side",
        "body": "Notice how the same idea shifts with formality. The negative tú and the usted forms both come from the subjunctive:",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>No hagas</b> eso. / <b>No haga</b> eso.",
            "en": "Don't do that. (friend / formal)",
            "note": "hagas = tú, haga = usted"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>No comas</b> ahí. / <b>No coma</b> ahí.",
            "en": "Don't eat there. (friend / formal)",
            "note": "comas = tú, coma = usted"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Negative tú uses the subjunctive: no hables, no comas, no escribas.",
      "Irregulars: no vayas (ir), no hagas (hacer).",
      "Usted commands use the same subjunctive form for yes and no: hable, coma, venga, haga; no hable.",
      "-ar verbs flip to -e(s), -er/-ir verbs flip to -a(s)."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "No habla tan rápido.",
        "r": "No hables tan rápido.",
        "why": "A negative tú command needs the subjunctive: no hables, not no habla."
      },
      {
        "w": "No comes eso.",
        "r": "No comas eso.",
        "why": "-er verbs flip to -as in the negative command: no comas."
      },
      {
        "w": "Habla más despacio, señor. (formal)",
        "r": "Hable más despacio, señor.",
        "why": "For usted, use the subjunctive form: hable, not habla."
      }
    ]
  },
